{
 "lead": "Noise is a random signal added to the true pixel value. Its probability density p(z) tells how likely each grey level z is. The shape of p(z) matters because a filter that suits Gaussian noise may fail on salt-and-pepper noise. Engineers measure the density on a flat patch, then choose a filter. This chapter lists the standard densities, their parameters, and the physical source of each.",
 "prereq": "You need: <a href=\"#image-degradation-and-restoration-model\">3.1</a> for where n enters the model, and Unit 2 histograms.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Additive noise model",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The observed pixel is g(x,y) = f(x,y) + n(x,y). f is the true grey level, n is a random noise value, and x,y are pixel coordinates. n is independent of f and changes from pixel to pixel. The same equation holds for all pixels, so we can drop coordinates and write g = f + n."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Multiplicative noise is different: g = f·n, common in radar and laser speckle. A log transform turns it into additive noise."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The noise is described by its probability density p(z). z is a grey level, and p(z)dz is the probability that n lies between z and z+dz. The mean µ = E[z] and variance σ² = E[(z−µ)²] summarise the centre and spread. A flat patch has almost constant f, so the histogram of that patch estimates p(z)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Periodic noise, such as a sine ripple from a power supply, is not random in this sense and does not fit these densities. It gets a frequency-domain treatment in <a href=\"#periodic-noise-reduction-in-the-frequency-domain\">3.4</a>."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Standard densities",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "In each row the parameters belong to that row only. For impulse noise, P_a + P_b = 1, and a and b are grey levels."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Density",
      "p(z)",
      "Mean",
      "Variance",
      "Typical source"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Gaussian",
       "1/(√(2π)σ) exp(−(z−µ)²/(2σ²))",
       "µ",
       "σ²",
       "Thermal sensor noise"
      ],
      [
       "Rayleigh",
       "(2/b)(z−a) exp(−(z−a)²/b), z≥a",
       "a+√(πb)/2",
       "b(4−π)/4",
       "Laser and ultrasound speckle"
      ],
      [
       "Gamma (Erlang)",
       "a^b z^(b−1) e^(−az)/(b−1)!, z≥0",
       "b/a",
       "b/a²",
       "Speckle with several scatterers"
      ],
      [
       "Exponential",
       "a e^(−az), z≥0",
       "1/a",
       "1/a²",
       "Rare bright events; gamma with b=1"
      ],
      [
       "Uniform",
       "1/(b−a), a≤z≤b",
       "(a+b)/2",
       "(b−a)²/12",
       "Quantisation"
      ],
      [
       "Impulse (bipolar)",
       "P_a at z=a, P_b at z=b",
       "P_a a + P_b b",
       "P_a P_b(a−b)²",
       "Faulty switching, dust"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Unipolar impulse noise has one impulse value a and clean pixels at c: put b=c and P_b=1−P in the bipolar row. Salt-and-pepper is bipolar with a=0 and b=255 in 8-bit images."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Estimating parameters from a flat patch",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Noise is measured where the true image is flat. A textured or edged region adds real variation and inflates the estimate."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "µ̂ = (1/N) Σ z_i,   σ̂² = (1/N) Σ (z_i − µ̂)²",
     "where": "z_i is the i-th pixel in the patch; N is the number of pixels; µ̂ is the sample mean; σ̂² is the sample variance."
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "Pick a region with no edges or texture, at least 20×20 pixels if possible.",
      "Plot its histogram; for a flat patch this histogram estimates p(z).",
      "Compute the sample mean and variance with the formulas above.",
      "Match the histogram shape: symmetric bell → Gaussian; one-sided tail with z≥a → Rayleigh or gamma; flat top → uniform; spikes at two levels → impulse.",
      "For a Gaussian, assume zero-mean noise, estimate the flat level by µ̂, and report σ̂ as the noise standard deviation. For other densities, solve the mean and variance equations for the PDF parameters."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The fitted density is only a model. If the histogram has isolated spikes, treat the image as having impulse noise first; a mean filter is the wrong tool there. Filters for each density are in <a href=\"#restoration-filters-mean-order-statistic-and-adaptive\">3.3</a>."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "figure": {
  "after_section": 1,
  "caption": "Three densities centred on zero: the narrow Gaussian has σ = 1, the wide one σ = 2, and the Laplace curve, p(z) = (1/2b) exp(−|z|/b) with b = 1, is more peaked with heavier tails. Laplace is drawn only for comparison, as a common model of heavy-tailed noise. The spread σ sets how far noise values stray from the mean.",
