  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Write the Gaussian noise density and name its two parameters.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "p(z) = 1/(√(2π)σ) exp(−(z−µ)²/(2σ²)). The parameters are the mean µ and the standard deviation σ; the variance is σ²."
  },
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "What are the two impulse values in 8-bit salt-and-pepper noise, and what does each look like?",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "The values are 0 (pepper, black) and 255 (salt, white). Bipolar impulse noise has probabilities P_a at one level and P_b at the other, with P_a + P_b = 1."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does quantisation produce approximately uniform noise?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Quantisation rounds a continuous value to the nearest level. The rounding error is roughly equally likely anywhere between −Δ/2 and +Δ/2, where Δ is the step between levels. A flat density over that interval is uniform."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why is a mean filter a poor choice for impulse noise?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "An impulse is a rare, extreme value. Its variance is not a useful measure of spread, and averaging spreads the extreme value over neighbouring pixels. A median or other order-statistic filter removes the impulse without blurring the edge."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "Exponential noise has p(z) = a e^(−az) for z≥0, with a = 0.2 grey-level⁻¹. Find its mean and variance.",
